                <pb facs="0190" n="78" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg78"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/1389: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Legation in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-17T12:00:00+08:00">March 17,
                        1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received March 17—3:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>50. On March 16, 8 p.m., the Ministry of Foreign Affairs delivered to this office
                    a memorandum dated March 16 enclosing a copy of a declaration in regard to the
                    reported sale of the Chinese Eastern Railway. The translation of the declaration
                    supplied by the Foreign Office concludes with the following paragraph: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“In conclusion, the Chinese Government emphatically declares that the
                            transfer, by sale or otherwise, of the Chinese Eastern Railway, which
                            entirely lies within China’s domain and the status of which is derived
                            from China’s special grant, is considered by the Chinese Government and
                            people as an unlawful transaction without any binding force and further,
                            as an international entity in respect of which the Chinese Government
                            fully reserves its rights.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Original will be mailed to the Legation today. The complete text has been
                    telegraphed by the Foreign Office to the Chinese Legation at Washington and will
                    be published in the newspapers March 19. Repeated to the Legation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
